<title>DENMARK: Danish Feb North Sea oil production fell 4.9 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish Feb North Sea oil production fell 4.9 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-03-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Danish Underground Consortium's (DUC) North Sea oil production fell 4.9 percent in February from January, an official at operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Friday.</p>
<p>It said oil production was 828,000 tonnes in February, or 217,246 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 228,352 bpd in January .</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually the entire output from Denmark's North Sea oil and gas fields. Denmark is the third biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain.  </p>
<p>Production data (figures in 1,000 bpd):</p>
<p>Field			Feb 97	    Jan 97	Feb 96</p>
<p>Dan			   69.8		69.5	  64.5 </p>
<p>Kraka			  5.6		 7.0	   7.1 </p>
<p>Regnar			 0.0		 0.0	   1.0 </p>
<p>Gorm			  46.8		48.9	  43.8 </p>
<p>Skjold			33.1		35.2	  38.2 </p>
<p>Rolf			   3.5		 4.2	   4.4 </p>
<p>Dagmar			 0.3		 0.3	   0.3 </p>
<p>Tyra			  25.9		23.7	  30.2 </p>
<p>Valdemar		     2.2		 2.8	   3.4 </p>
<p>Roar			   9.7		 8.5	   3.0</p>
<p>Svend			 20.2		28.2	    -- </p>
<p>Total			217.2	     228.4	 196.0</p>
<p>A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners, Royal Dutch/Shell group and Texaco Inc own 46 percent and 15 percent respectively.</p>
<p>-- Steve Weizman, Copenhagen newsroom +45-33-969652</p>
